  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=-4{e}^{2-x}$ at the point where $x=2-ln\u2061(3).$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-4{e}^{u},\\,u=2-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2-ln\u2061(3)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=2-ln\u2061(3)} & =4{e}^{2-(2-ln\u2061(3))} \\\\  & =4{e}^{ln\u2061(3)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
